# Slice 334 (Codex review of 333, item 1): descriptions of functions written in the data part of main, each with the
# field name -- the second a descriptor without a body -- compose by the same rules as any Structure: the part args is
# composed (b takes d2's default 4), name and return are definitions the later operand writes, the part code stays d1's
# (it is not glued). `fn: h merge(d1, d2)` names the function h (the field name of the parts is an ordinary field); the
# whole-Structure form `fn: merge(d1, d2)` takes the field name of its composition, two. d1 and d2 stay as written;
# neither is a function.
# Plan M11 5v: the declarations of ordinary Structures live in the data part of main; the root holds places, the
# receivers `@` and `fn` that name them, and the call main(). The places d1_b, d2_b, d2_args, d1_parts and d2_parts keep
# what the dump of d1 and d2 showed: they stay as written.
sub: main () (
    d1:
        name: one
        args: (int: a; int: b 1)
        return: int
        code: (return: a * 10 + b)
    d2:
        name: two
        args: (int: b 4)
        return: int
    int: r1 0
    int: r2 0
    int: r3 0
)
    r1 := h(1)
    r2 := two(2)
    r3 := h(1; 7)
@: d1 main\data\d1
@: d2 main\data\d2
fn: h merge(d1, d2)
fn: merge(d1, d2)
main()
int: r1 main\data\r1
int: r2 main\data\r2
int: r3 main\data\r3
int: d1_b d1\args\b
int: d2_b d2\args\b
int: d2_args length(d2\args)
int: d1_parts length(d1)
int: d2_parts length(d2)
